{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And you shall know this day — but not your children who did not know.</b> Scripture says: these awesome things that I am recounting are not for the future, for your children to see them; for you yourselves saw them with your own eyes. And everything flows into the verse ״כי עיניכם הרואות״ — for your eyes have seen — that is, you are witnesses to this matter. And he reminded them of the well-known miracles, which are the miracles of Egypt, as he said, ״ואת אותותיו ואת מעשיו אשר עשה בתוך מצרים לפרעה״, and the matter of the splitting of the Sea of Reeds is what he said: ״אשר הציף את מי ים סוף״, and the matter of the manna is what he said: ״ואשר עשה לכם במדבר״. And the reason he did not mention the descent of the manna explicitly is so that he could include in this the miracles of the quail and the well. And he mentioned the opening of the earth, which is what he said: ״ואשר עשה לדתן ולאבירם״. And he should have said ״ואשר עשה לקרח״, for he was the chief of the dispute, but Moses did not mention him out of humility, for he was ashamed of the sin of that man who was close to him; or he wished to mention these because they were more brazen than all of them, as they came out and stood defiantly and were reviling and blaspheming, as the Sages, of blessed memory, mentioned." "<b>And you already</b> know that the hidden miracles are from the power of El Shaddai, which is the grasp of the patriarchs — it is the honored Name about which it is written (Deuteronomy 28:58): ״ליראה את השם הנכבד והנורא הזה את HaShem אלהיך״; and the publicized miracles are from the power of the designated Name, the great Name — it is the grasp of Moses, peace be upon him. Therefore you will find at the beginning of the parashah in the bodily blessings that they are all hidden miracles, where he mentions ״HaShem אלהיך״ as is his practice throughout the repetition of the Torah (Deuteronomy — this is what he said (Deuteronomy 11:7): ״ושמר HaShem אלהיך לך״; but here, where he mentioned the publicized miracles, he mentioned the great Name, as he said: ״כי עיניכם הרואות את כל מעשה HaShem הגדול אשר עשה״. And understand this." "<b>His greatness, His mighty hand, and His outstretched arm.</b> It is known to every understanding person that the terms 'hand' and 'arm' do not apply to the Holy One, blessed be He, but they are matters above that are likened to hand and arm, and those matters are forces and attributes. And you should not understand that both of them are said of one single power; rather, each and every one is a force in its own right. For so it is in man: 'hand' and 'arm' are not said of one limb alone, for each is a limb in its own right. For what is called 'hand' (יד) is the palm of the hand, in which are five fingers up to the third joint — where the palm of the hand ends — it is called יד. From there onward up to the elbow, which is called מרפק and in the foreign tongue קובדי״ר, it is called קנה, for it is the shaft connected with the palm of the hand. From the elbow and upward it is called זרוע, and it is the place of the laying of tefillin, which is the upper part of the arm, and it is opposite the heart. Concerning this they said in tractate Menaḥot: 'Beloved are Israel before the Place, blessed be He, for He gave them tefillin on their arms and tefillin on their heads.' And a proof that what is after the elbow is called זרוע is what Job said (Job 31:22): ״כתפי משכמה תפול ואזרועי מקנה תשבר״ — he began from the shoulder, which is above, and afterward זרוע, and he said מקנה, which is what is connected with the palm of the hand. And so we learned in tractate Oholot, where it counts the 248 limbs in man, and it says there: thirty in the palm of the hand, six in each and every finger, two in the קנה, two in the מרפק, one in the זרוע, and four in the shoulder. And this is a proof that what is connected with the palm of the hand is called קנה. And the term עציל is called in the language of the Beraita, and the term מרפק in the language of the Mishnah, and אציל in the language of Scripture, as it is written (Ezekiel 13:18): ״על כל אצילי ידי״, and Jonathan translated: ״על כל מרפקי ידוי״. And in tractate Soferim they said: 'A person should not place a Torah scroll on his knees and put his two אצילין upon it, because it constitutes a demeaning practice toward it.' Now that this distinction between יד and זרוע has been clarified, we may say that it is a well-known thing that the זרוע is greater than the יד, and the power of the hand is only through the power of the arm." "<b>From this point on</b> it will be explained to you: every place in the Torah where יד and זרוע are said of the Holy One, blessed be He — all of it is said concerning the attribute of justice (din). But among the parts of justice there is justice that is harsher than its fellow and justice that is lighter than its fellow, one above the other and one below the other, just as among the shades of wine or blood there is a red appearance one above the other and one below the other. And so here: the יד is a weak attribute of justice, and the weak force is only through the strong force, as I hinted regarding the two letters ה of (Exodus 15:11) ״מי כמוכה״. And this is (Isaiah 53:1): ״וזרוע HaShem על מי נגלתה״, and this is (Psalms 44:4): ״כי ימינך וזרועך״. And I already hinted at this in the verse (Exodus 14:31): ״וירא ישראל את היד הגדולה״." "<b>That</b> he mentioned here ״את גדלו״ and afterward said ״את ידו החזקה וזרועו הנטויה״ — it is like (Deuteronomy 5:21): ״הן הראנו HaShem אלהינו את כבודו ואת גדלו״, for ״ידו החזקה וזרועו הנטויה״ is His glory, which is the weak attribute of justice receiving from the arm. Upon it the designated Name is inscribed, to indicate that mercy is within it, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 26): ״כי ביה HaShem״. And so you will find in the prayer of Solomon, peace be upon him, who said (I Kings 8:42): ״כי ישמעון את שמך הגדול ואת ידך החזקה וזרועך הנטויה״ — he mentioned first the attribute of mercy and afterward the attribute of justice, within which is mercy. And this is clear."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:42.999282080Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}